    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[26, 6, 27], [5, 9, 30], [28, 15, 13], [22, 23, 26], [19, 30, 9]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 2], [0, 1, 2], [1, 2, 0], [2, 0, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Production Leadership Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated Scheduling of Jobs Across Specialized Workstations\nDate: [Today]\n\nOverview\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that support discrete product builds. The current production plan includes several customer jobs, each of which follows a fixed routing with a set of operations that must be completed in sequence. Every operation is tied to a designated machine and has a defined processing duration.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nBecause multiple jobs share the same machines at different points in their routings, we face contention for capacity. The order in which we load operations on each machine affects downstream availability for subsequent steps within the same job and can create waiting time on other workstations. Processing times vary across operations, which means some tasks are brief while others are comparatively longer, making it harder to balance flow and avoid idle time or bottlenecks. The practical result is that local choices on one machine can ripple across the shop, extending the overall time to finish all work if not sequenced thoughtfully.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Machine exclusivity: Each workstation can process a single operation at a time; there is no overlap of operations on the same machine.\n- Fixed job routing: Within each job, operations must be executed in the specified order; no operation may begin before its predecessor in that job finishes.\n- Designated resources and known durations: Every operation must run on its assigned machine for its known processing time.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption or splitting across time blocks.\n- Resource readiness and handoffs: An operation may only start when both its machine is free and all prior operations in its job have been completed.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, conflict-free schedule that respects all routing and machine-capacity rules while completing the full set of jobs in the shortest total time (minimizing overall makespan). In practice, this means sequencing work on each machine to reduce waiting, maintain flow, and avoid creating new bottlenecks downstream.\n\nNext Steps\n- Confirm the routing and timing details for each job\u2019s operations (see Annex).\n- Generate a machine-by-machine sequence that aligns with the fixed job orders and non-interruption rule.\n- Review the resulting plan to identify critical operations, expected idle periods, and opportunities to compress the overall completion time.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 5\n- Number of machines: 3\n- Processing times by job and operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 26, Op2: 6, Op3: 27]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 5, Op2: 9, Op3: 30]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 28, Op2: 15, Op3: 13]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 22, Op2: 23, Op3: 26]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 19, Op2: 30, Op3: 9]\n- Machine assignments by job and operation: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 1]",
